{"id":"h277","run":2,"role":"test_wrong","category":"science-chemistry","question":"Carbon dioxide is reduced by hydrogen according to the equation CO_2(g) + H_2(g) \\rightleftarrows CO(g) + H_2O(g) . One mole of CO_2 is reacted with one mole of H_2 at 825K and it is found that, at equilibrium, 0.27atmof CO is present. Determine the equilibrium constant, K, at 825K .\nIs the correct answer \"0.137\"?","truth":"yes","model_answer":"no","correct":false,"source":"TIGER-Lab/MMLU-Pro, test split","claims":[{"text":"The correct equilibrium constant is about 1.38, not 0.137.","confidence":0.9,"lean":-1,"theta":1.4137,"phi":3.1416},{"text":"0.137 matches 0.27²/0.73², which would follow only if CO were 0.27 mol out of 1 mol total.","confidence":0.8,"lean":0.0,"theta":1.2566,"phi":-0.0},{"text":"That mole accounting does not fit this reaction, so the 0.137 route is invalid.","confidence":0.85,"lean":-1,"theta":1.3352,"phi":3.1416},{"text":"Stoichiometry gives x mol CO and H2O and (1−x) mol each of CO2 and H2 at equilibrium.","confidence":0.95,"lean":-1,"theta":1.4923,"phi":3.1416},{"text":"Total moles stay at 2 regardless of x, since gas moles are equal on both sides.","confidence":0.95,"lean":-1,"theta":1.4923,"phi":3.1416},{"text":"Because gas moles are equal on both sides, K does not depend on total pressure.","confidence":0.85,"lean":0.0,"theta":1.3352,"phi":3.1416},{"text":"The problem does not state the total pressure, so 1 atm must be assumed.","confidence":0.8,"lean":-1,"theta":1.2566,"phi":3.1416},{"text":"At 1 atm total pressure, CO mole fraction is 0.27, so x/2 = 0.27 and x = 0.54.","confidence":0.9,"lean":-1,"theta":1.4137,"phi":3.1416},{"text":"Partial pressures are 0.27 atm for CO and H2O and 0.23 atm for CO2 and H2.","confidence":0.9,"lean":-1,"theta":1.4137,"phi":3.1416},{"text":"K = 0.27²/0.23² ≈ 1.38.","confidence":0.9,"lean":-1,"theta":1.4137,"phi":3.1416}],"relations":[{"i":0,"j":1,"J":0.5,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":2,"J":0.6,"relation":"supports"},